Fillets: an analytic blend whose contact on one face runs into the face at
an end of the spine before that end is cut by that face all along, up to
where its other contact reaches it (mitcad#121): e.g. the foot of the end
wall of a groove whose floor rounds up into the part's side (a concave
arc, then a convex arc tangent to the side); towards the side the wall is
lower than the rounding, whose contact on the wall runs past the wall's
top edge into the side.

- ChFi3d_Builder::PerformSetOfKPart: after the gaps over holes (0055,
  0057), ChFi3d_KPartCutToEnd looks at each end of an open spine whose
  edges all have analytic blends. Where the piece of SplitKPart nearest
  to the end has its contact on face N leaving N across an edge into the
  face B that holds the spine's end vertex V, and the edges between have
  no piece (their contact lies off N), pieces cut by B are added from that
  point to the end (ChFi3d_KPartCutPiece, as over a hole: the side on N
  replaced by the blend's intersection with B), each edge's own blend on
  each edge. Before, the rest was walked rolling on N's edge with B (OCCT's
  obstacle), which got stuck where the other contact passes onto a tangent
  face, and the corner at V failed (IntersectionAtEnd : the max number of
  faces reached).
- The other contact of the end edge's blend runs into B at the edge Eq
  between its face and B at V; there the cut meets it and the blend ends
  in a point. The point is found on Eq (GeomAPI_ExtremaCurveCurve), its
  section by bisection along the end edge, and the last piece's end is
  given as that point on Eq on both sides (ChFi3d_KPartCutEnd). The
  spine's end becomes a free boundary with a singular extremity: no
  corner is made at V (ChFi3d_Builder::PerformFilletOnVertex), the
  stripe's end has its point and no end curve (ChFi3d_SingularExtremity
  for a point that is no vertex, where both contacts meet at one point of
  the blend's surface), and ChFi3d_FilDS adds no end edge there but cuts
  the edges the end point lies on.
- ChFi3d_KPartCutPiece: a vertex of B on its boundary with N counts as
  under the blend (refusing the cut) only where an edge of B other than
  its edges with N ends there; a vertex between two edges of B and N needs
  no edge grown. The spine's end vertex itself is no such vertex.
- Where the piece nearest to the end starts at the start of its edge, the
  pieces before it lie on the blends of other edges: their common end is
  given on those blends' surfaces.
